Q: org.openqa.selenium.SessionNotCreatedException: Could not start a new session. Response code 500. Message: Failed to create Chrome process A: <p>A bit unclear why you want to use <em>chromedriver 145.0.7632.26</em>.</p>
<p>You can use <em>chromedriver 145.0.7632.26</em> once the published <a href="/questions/tagged/google-chrome" class="s-tag post-tag" title="show questions tagged &#39;google-chrome&#39;" aria-label="show questions tagged &#39;google-chrome&#39;" rel="tag" aria-labelledby="tag-google-chrome-tooltip-container" data-tag-menu-origin="Unknown">google-chrome</a> browser version is bumped to v145.x, the current release being <em>Version 144.0.7559.110 (Official Build)</em>.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Solution</h2>
<p>Using <em>Google Chrome</em> browser <em>Version 144.0.7559.110 (Official Build)</em> and Selenium <em>v4.6</em> or above you can leverage the help offered through <a href="https://www.selenium.dev/documentation/selenium_manager/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Selenium Manager</a> a tool implemented in Rust to allow cross-platform execution and compiled for Windows, Linux, and macOS. The Selenium Manager binaries are shipped with every Selenium release. Using <em>Selenium Manager</em> you won't have to think about using or implementing <a href="https://bonigarcia.dev/webdrivermanager/" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><em>WebDriverMananger</em></a> anymore.</p>
<p>Your minimal code lines will be:</p>
<pre><code>package newPackage;

import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;
import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;

public class ChromeInitialization {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        WebDriver driver = new ChromeDriver();
        driver.get(&quot;https://www.google.com/&quot;);

    }
}
</code></pre>
